The '''2026 Portblanc fires''' were a {{w|grass fire}} that burned through the eastern districts of [[Portblanc]], the capital of [[Tramuntana Province]], [[Balboa]], from 22 to 24 February 2026. The fire destroyed 36 houses and caused three minor injuries, all among residents of the affected area. The fire was caused by an {{w|electrical fault}} in an informal agricultural settlement on the eastern fringe of the city, where an improvised power connection to an irrigation pump short-circuited and ignited dry grassland during the dry season.

== Background ==

The eastern districts of Portblanc, unlike the northwestern hills affected by the [[2024 Portblanc fires|2024 fires]], are situated on the relatively flat coastal plain between the city centre and the surrounding agricultural land. The area includes a mix of planned residential developments and informal agricultural settlements — smallholdings established by seasonal workers and tenant farmers who had built dwellings and irrigation infrastructure without formal permits. The informal settlements relied on improvised electrical connections to the city's power grid, which were not subject to inspection or safety regulation.

The 2025–2026 dry season was again below average, though not as severe as the preceding year. The grassland on the eastern fringe of the city had dried sufficiently by late February to present a fire risk, particularly in the areas where residential and agricultural land use interfaced with uncultivated grassland.

== Fires ==

The fire began on the morning of 22 February 2026, when an electrical short circuit in an improvised power connection to an irrigation pump in an informal agricultural settlement on the eastern fringe of Portblanc ignited the surrounding dry grass. The fire spread eastward through the grassland, driven by light winds, and reached the nearest residential properties within approximately two hours.

The fire burned through 36 houses in the eastern district, the majority of which were informal dwellings constructed from lightweight materials that offered little resistance to the fire. The relatively flat terrain and lighter winds, compared to the 2024 fires, allowed emergency services to contain the fire more quickly. The fire was brought under control on 24 February, within two days of ignition.

Three minor injuries were reported, all among residents of the affected settlement who suffered smoke inhalation and minor burns while attempting to salvage belongings. No fatalities occurred.

== Aftermath ==

The 2026 fires, though significantly smaller in scale than the [[2024 Portblanc fires|2024 fires]], drew attention to the fire risk posed by informal settlements on the periphery of the city, where improvised electrical infrastructure and lightweight construction compounded the danger of grass fire ignition and spread. The Tramuntana Provincial Assembly's 2024 fire safety legislation had focused on the hillside areas of the coastal Tramuntana range and had not addressed the risks associated with informal agricultural settlements on the coastal plain.

The fires prompted calls for the regularisation of electrical connections in informal settlements, the extension of firebreak requirements to the agricultural-urban interface on the eastern fringe of the city, and the provision of fire-resistant building materials to residents of informal settlements. The provincial government announced a review of informal settlement safety standards in March 2026, though no legislation had been enacted by the time of the [[2026 Balboa data center protests|August 2026 data center protests]], which displaced the fires from the political agenda.

